  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 224 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nA slowly building area of large scale ridging will lead to \npersistent upper-level northwest flow into the Midwest region \nthroughout the remainder of the work week. Dry conditions are \nexpected to keep hold, with warming temperatures Tuesday as \nincreasing 850mb temps move into the state, especially west around \n18-20C, and increasing winds from a tightened pressure gradient and \nefficient daytime mixing, leading to highs in the mid to upper 70s \nacross Central Iowa. An area of Canadian high pressure sinks down \ninto the region once again by midweek, with temperatures a touch \ncooler in the low to mid 70s forecast as winds shift \nnorth/northwesterly. By Thursday into Friday, a weak shortwave \ndescending the eastern fringe of the aforementioned ridge looks to \nmake its way into Iowa, with a return of mid-level moisture and \nforcing that will introduce the next potential for rain activity. \nThe GFS is showing coverage over the southwest portion of the state \nThursday evening into Friday, with dry conditions elsewhere given \nthe lingering area of high pressure centered over the Ohio Valley. \nThe Euro however has a direct west to east passage of this activity \nacross Central Iowa Thursday afternoon to evening. Certainly notable \ndifferences between long range models, but consensus on rain \nreturning somewhere nonetheless, so will have to monitor trends over \nthe next several days to better pinpoint coverage and timing \ndetails. Late week into the weekend shows a building ridge over the \ncentral US, with temperatures warming overhead as highs in the mid \nto upper 70s are expected, along with a return of dry conditions \nonce again, but also notably breezy.",
